# China Beneath the Shadow of the Red Flag

Shanghai—the city praised as ancient and distinguished, the largest in Asia, known as the fourth largest in the world, flourishing in commercial wealth, distributing a multitude of goods, containing six million people, expanding manufacturing industry, spreading its working population across three square miles, and serving as the central port of the Eastern countries—has fallen into the embrace of warriors bearing the red flag.

The principal city of Chiang's regime has fallen into the hands of the Chinese Communists.

While Shanghai's outskirts became a field of corpses and the stench of death blew towards the city, Shanghai itself leapt into Communist hands.

Bombs split the sky, conches of victory sounded, heroic cries rose from the field, and crowds of people pressed close behind. Soldiers dressed in green-tinted khaki entered Shanghai bearing the red flag. Every government official who had ruled the city until then boarded aircraft overnight and flew swiftly away, seeking the outside world.

The Communists, who had advanced by fighting every step of the way, captured Shanghai peacefully. When a siege is broken and an opposing army enters a city, its people generally become terrified and deeply disturbed. In Shanghai, however, because the Communists carried out the capture peacefully and in an orderly manner, joy rather than fear dances among the people.

As Communist forces entered Shanghai, Dr Wu Yen, a political intellectual who had served as an envoy to the Communists two years earlier, asked the city's six million people to remain calm and continue their usual work. He also urged workers not to engage in any destruction but to concentrate upon their jobs.

The people welcomed the peace. They stood at their doorways and welcomed the change. Students marched in procession and sang songs of victory.

The chief city has fallen. It is now certain that the parts of southern China that have not yet fallen will soon do so. The red colour spreading down from the north will now cover the south as well, and all China will stand clothed in red. That spectacle will plunge the world's great powers into profound thought and will undoubtedly give the dollar chain a vigorous shake.

Chiang Kai-shek, formerly head of the Chinese government and now in hiding, and his Kuomintang did not respect the people; they began to forget them. The people in turn began to forget a leader and party that did not respect them.

That is why government forces began to dissolve while Communist forces began to grow. A remarkable process occurred: the weapons America had supplied to government troops gradually passed into the hands of the Communists advancing against them. The rulers failed to respect the people; the people joined the opposing side; those who governed arrogantly have now bitten the dust.

A few weeks ago, a commander resisting the siege proclaimed that they would make Shanghai "a second Stalingrad." At Stalingrad, the people fought Hitler's forces street by street and house by house. That struggle became a turning point in the world war. The vigorous Russians attacked the Nazis, drove them back as far as Berlin, and defeated them there.

Did such a condition prevail in Shanghai? No. On the contrary, the city's internal security forces welcomed the arriving Communists. They removed the barricades erected in the streets and tore down posters placed on walls against the Communists. It appears that the Nationalist government hoped to win through empty words alone and had lost, many days earlier, any intention of recovering the city through the power of resistance.

The Communists stand united under the leadership of Mao Tse-tung. Although Mao now celebrates victory, numerous problems confront him. He bears the great responsibility of removing the incapacity and corruption that have prevailed until now and establishing an efficient government founded upon social equality.

We believe that the victorious Communists will labour to establish good self-government free from exploitation and raise China to a high position on the world stage in the social, economic, and political spheres.

Some fear that China will now become a slave to Moscow.

Whether China becomes Moscow's slave or lives as its friend depends upon the character of the government Mao Tse-tung demonstrates in practice. We too must wait and see.

Shanghai beneath the shadow of the red flag! Red across the expanse of China! As the red rises, may happiness blossom in the people's hearts!

29 May 1949

---NOTES---
This is a contemporary political assessment written during the Communist capture of Shanghai. Reports of public reaction, the causes of Nationalist defeat, and forecasts for Communist rule remain attributed to the article's 1949 viewpoint. Several names and phrases are OCR-damaged; English uses historically identifiable forms where secure and logs them separately.
